{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S2oMr3noAsXjpNipQD1hmo","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 16:10 +07\n2\t# Phase 7 plan: Stage D/E rerun over the row carrier (Architect, plan only)\n3\t\n4\tAuthor: claude-fable-5-1. Worktree `srvm-phase7-plan` at `781a9ba8` (phases 1-6 merged). Read: the four design documents named in the brief, `rows.cljc`, `handoff.cljc` (export-task, the reader, resume-task*, the recovery paths), `holder/{export,driver,writer,reader}.cljc`, `checkpoint.cljc`, `custody.cljc`, `ledger.cljc`, `authority/{grant,completion,admission}.cljc`, `compose.cljc`, the Stage E world, scenarios, peer, process test and rows test, the checkpoint and ledger fixtures and tests, `lift_support.cljc`, `handoff_v2_holder_test.cljc`, the semantic-register safepoint harness, `dao.jing.{file,mem}` put contracts, `yin.repl`'s composition wiring. No code or doc was edited.\n5\t\n6\t## Summary for the owner (15 lines)\n7\t\n8\t1. Every Stage D/E test today runs on the **old** `yin.vm.semantic` kernel through `handoff.cljc` bodies (version 0 stream-CBOR, version 1 and 2 jing-CBOR); the row carrier (version 3) runs only on the semantic-register kernel and only with `custody nil`.\n9\t2. Phase 7 is therefore one move, done in four slices: teach the row carrier custody, re-base the holder (export record, recovery object, driver lower) and the authority's inspector on row packs, then rerun the composition and the Stage E crash matrix on the semantic-register kernel, three hosts.\n10\t3. The driver, writer, reader, compose, authority, ledger and front are **kernel-agnostic**: they read machine fields that `yin.vm.engine` owns (`:wait-set`, `:installs`, `:yin.k/gate`, `:yin.k/custody`). They change only where they call `handoff/*` or `checkpoint/inspect` (eight call sites).\n11\t4. Nothing of version 0/1/2 is rewritten; it dies at phase 8. Phase 7 deletes only what it replaces: the version-1 export-recovery object, the holder's use of `export-task`/`resume-task`, the authority's use of the version-1 inspector. The old codec and its direct tests stay green and dead until phase 8.\n12\t5. One hard fact drives the design: `dao.jing` content stores **verify bytes against the address on put**, and a row pack's bytes do not hash to its root id. So the checkpoint has two identities: the pack address (storage, the ledger's `:yin.k/id`) and the root id (semantic, inside the baseline). `emit` must sort its rows so one root gives one pack on every host.\n13\t6. Custody travels as the root's `custody` slot exactly as the draft's §5.5 table says (policy, occurrence, arbitration, next-op-seq, origin); lease, epoch and holder never enter a body (UCF 7.2.1 stands); op-ids already have their slots on `:put`, `:ffi-request` and `:link-request` pendings.\n14\t7. The recovery object loses its second \"snapshot\" body (rows already carry the whole store and every parked record) and keeps only holder-local engine state (gate, issues, issued, origins) beside the pack.\n15\t8. Crash cuts stay meaningful unchanged if the pack is stored as **one** blob; storing rows individually would add a torn-pack cut family and is not recommended.\n16\t9. `:resume-mode` stays out: the version-2 stack lane is frozen until phase 8, and rows carry `:act`/`:tail`, which subsume it. No work.\n17\t10. Three questions need the owner: fork spelling in the custody slot (nil as landed, or the draft's explicit `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}`); the REPL custody gap (the REPL composes the old kernel until phase 8, so its custody path is dead between 7 and 8 unless the kernel switch is pulled forward); whether the Q3 install checks (link-id = parent, image address = manifest) land in 7b.\n18\t11. Slices: 7a custody on rows and the rows inspector with regenerated fixtures; 7b holder export, recovery v2 and the driver's lower; 7c the composition and the compose/fencing row tests; 7d the Stage E world and matrix across processes; 7e docs. 7a → 7b → 7c → 7d, with 7e and the 7d world port overlapping 7c.\n19\t12. Estimate: 12 working days median (9 implementation, the rest 2-4 codex rounds per slice and the slow-tier runs), 17 at the worst; phase 6's three slices landed in three calendar days with the design pre-written, so calendar could be shorter.\n20\t13. Biggest risks: the identity split surprising the ledger's variant rules (mitigated by sorting rows); the fenced-wait rehydration of install children on the semantic-register kernel, exercised only by the harness today; Dart runs only the in-process Stage E form (as today).\n21\t14. Nothing found that forces a redesign of rows or the ledger; the de Bruijn lanes are not Stage E participants (the 61/350 matrix covers only the cross-engine laws).\n22\t15. Verdict at the end: **NOT READY** until the owner answers the three questions in §3.10; everything else is decided below and can start on 7a the moment the fork-spelling answer lands.\n23\t\n24\t---\n25\t\n26\t## 1. Inventory: every Stage D/E scenario and test, its transport today, what it needs on rows\n27\t\n28\tVocabulary (UCF 7.5.3): a *cursor* is the immutable `dao.stream` value; a *cursor cell* is the carrier's box (`:yin.k/cc-<n>` in rows, `:yin.k/c-<n>` in versions 0-2); a *cursor-ref* is the sealed reference a program holds; a *cell* is only the heap box.\n29\t\n30\tTransport codes: **v0** = `handoff/export-task` with a nil header, stream CBOR, fork; **v1** = the same with a header, jing CBOR, exclusive; **v2** = `{:version 2}`, the four-profile body (frozen); **rec1** = the export-recovery object version 1 (`holder.export/freeze`, embeds a v0/v1/v2 body plus a full-census snapshot); **ckpt** = `checkpoint/inspect` over v1/v2 bytes; **ledger** = `yin.vm.ucf.ledger` records (no body); **rows** = `rows/lift-task`/`lower-task`, version 3.\n31\t\n32\t### 1.1 Stage E (the acceptance matrix, `stage_e_*`)\n33\t\n34\tThe world (`stage_e_world.cljc`) builds both machines with `lift-support/new-machine`, which is `yin.vm.semantic/create-vm` (old kernel). Every life drives `compose` only; the export is v1 (`:export-version` nil), the recovery object rec1, the offer carries v1 bytes, the authority inspects with ckpt. Dart runs the in-process form only; JVM and Node also run the separate-process form over the four ordered pairs.\n35\t\n36\t| Scenario (ids) | Count | Parking shape (continuation §5.2 row) | Transport today | Needs on rows |\n37\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n38\t| `[:fork]` | 1 | row 1 (blocked read, `:parked` world) | v0 lift, no bracket | rows lift with no header → root custody nil (or fork spelling, §3.1); the driver's `fork-step` answers pack bytes and address |\n39\t| `[:row-7 cut]` × 5 cuts | 5 | row 1 at the source; successor is the same shape | v1 export, rec1, ckpt on offer and report | rows export under header; recovery v2; rows inspector on offer and report; successor pack address as `:yin.k/result` |\n40\t| `[:row-7 :failure-lower]`, `[:row-7 :lost-authority]` | 2 | row 1 | v1, ckpt | as above; failure-lower is `attach!` returning not-found inside `lower-task` step 11 |\n41\t| `[:row-4 cut]` × 3 | 3 | row 1 then row 2 (`:rows` world: read, write to the enrolled target, write to the side log, halt) | v1 with an op-id on the `:put`, ckpt baseline, admission | rows lift with the enrolled set (new `:unprotected-pending`/unenrolled checks); op-id slot already exists; baseline from the rows inspector |\n42\t| `[:row-3 variant]` × 2 | 2 | rows 1, 2 | v1, admission | as row 4 |\n43\t| `[:row-5 variant]` × 2 | 2 | rows 1, 2; evicted kept cursor (gap) | v1; input records | unchanged protocol; the kept cursor position is a cursor-cell body `[stream position]` in rows |\n44\t| `[:row-2 cut]` × 14 cuts | 14 | row 1 | v1 export phases: mint, serve, store recovery (`store-put-*`), store body (`body-put-*`), fence, offer | the same phases over the pack; see §3.4 for why the cut set stays |\n45\t| `[:row-2-carrier outcome]` × 4 | 4 | row 1 | v1 offer appends, abort | unchanged (`export/abort` is machine-level) |\n46\t| `[:row-1]` | 1 | row 1; a second encoding minted by rewriting `:yin.k/id-counter` in the CBOR body | v1 variant bytes | the variant must be re-emitted: decode pack → change the root's id-counter slot → `emit` → new root id and new pack; equal baseline (§3.3) |\n47\t| `[:row-6 ...]` × 3 | 3 | row 1 | v1; partition and lost reply | unchanged |\n48\t| `[:clause-8 variant]` × 2, `[:clause-8 :closed]` | 3 | rows 1, 2; cross-target | v1, admission, inherited ids read the accepted checkpoint through ckpt | `accepted-baseline` must inspect the stored pack with the rows inspector |\n49\t| `[:clause-3 :regrant]`, `[:clause-3 :carried-put]` | 2 | row 2 with an id assigned before export | v1 carries the id | rows carry `:op-id` on the `:put` pending slot; the successor's counter from the custody slot |\n50\t| `[:journal cut]` × 7, `{:fail ...}` × 2 | 9 | row 1 | v1; inbox retention, journal brackets | unchanged except the fence record's two addresses are recovery v2 and the pack |\n51\t\n52\tTotal: 51 scenarios (`all-scenarios`), 12 `deftest`s in `stage_e_rows_test` (8 slow) and 12 in `stage_e_process_test` (all slow, JVM only, four host pairs, Node peer compiled by shadow with the peer as main).\n53\t\n54\t**What the world itself needs:** `parked-reader-over` and `reading-writer` built with `yin.vm.semantic-register/create-vm` and `sr/load-vector` over `linearize/project` (as the harness's `machine`/`load-program` do), a `:source-index` supplied to the lift (the composition's `{A #{tree}}`, continuation §6.1), `holder-seams` unchanged, and `offer-variant!` re-emitting a pack instead of patching CBOR.\n55\t\n56\t### 1.2 Stage D (the driver, the holder halves, the compose rows)\n57\t\n58\t| Test file (deftests) | Transport today | What it tests | Needs on rows |\n59\t|---|---|---|---|\n60\t| `holder/driver_test.cljc` (118) | v0/v1/v2 through `handoff/resume-task`, `export/*`, rec1, ckpt | the whole driver: brackets, inbox, grant, lower, run, exit, reopen, abort, enrollment | machines from a semantic-register support namespace; the three driver call sites (`lower`, `step-validating`, `rebuilt-cell`/`reopen-holder`/`reopen-exit`) over rows; `a-version-two-fork-lift-is-the-composition-choice-test` deleted at 7 (its subject is the frozen v2 lane; nothing else exercises `:export-version 2` through the driver) |\n61\t| `holder/export_test.cljc` (26) | v0/v1/v2 enter/prepare/encode/freeze/rehydrate/abort | the exporting state and recovery | `enter`, `abort`, hold refusals: unchanged (machine-level); prepare/encode/freeze/rehydrate: rewritten over rows and recovery v2; the \"snapshot extends body\" and \"transitive module store fixed point\" tests disappear (rows carry everything once) |\n62\t| `holder/writer_test.cljc` (29) | machines with a hand-set `:yin.k/custody` map; no transport | assign, envelope, retention, discharge, closes | port the machine builders to the semantic-register kernel; logic unchanged (`writer.cljc` reads engine fields only) |\n63\t| `holder/reader_test.cljc` (24) | same | held observations, replay, frontier | same |\n64\t| `holder/evidence_test.cljc` (10) | ledger | binding evidence | none |\n65\t| `fencing_rows_test.cljc` (4) | v1 lift + writer + reader + authority, no compose | rows 2, 4, 5 fencing halves | rows lift under header; `lower-task` with the grant opts |\n66\t| `compose_test.cljc` (13) | v0/v1 through compose | open!, fork label, substrate checks, reopen, the plain composition | the `:receiver` and `:machine` seams become semantic-register machines; `the-plain-composition-loads-and-drives-with-no-yin-repl-test` reads the REPL's wiring (see §3.10 Q2) |\n67\t| `compose_rows_test.cljc` (41, 1 slow) | v1 through compose; rec1; ckpt | rows 1-8 same host, completion recovery, journal cuts around freeze/storage/fencing, the content-store cuts | as compose; the content-store cut tests must see two puts (recovery v2, pack) as today |\n68\t| `lift_v1_test.cljc` (29) | v1 `export-task` | header rules, enrolled refusals, canonical bytes, cursor cells | **kept until phase 8** (tests the old codec directly); its custody rules are re-pinned on rows in a new `rows_custody_test` (7a) |\n69\t| `handoff_v2_custody_test.cljc` (5), `handoff_v2_holder_test.cljc` (6), the other `handoff_v2_*` (8 files), `handoff_test`, `handoff_v1_test`, `scalar_round_trip_test` | v0/v1/v2 codec | the old codec and layouts | kept until phase 8, except `a-version-2-recovery-snapshot-freezes-and-rehydrates-fenced` (tests rec1 freeze, deleted at 7) and `a-poisoned-receiver-behaves-as-a-clean-one` (goes through `resume-task` only, keep) |\n70\t\n71\t### 1.3 Stage C (the authority), where a body enters\n72\t\n73\t| Test file (deftests) | Body use today | Needs on rows |\n74\t|---|---|---|\n75\t| `checkpoint_fixtures.cljc`, `checkpoint_test.cljc` (14), `test/resources/.../checkpoint-v1.txt` | five bases lifted by the old semantic kernel through v1, 48 mutations, 3 byte anchors | **kept as the old inspector's test** until phase 8; a new `rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc` + `rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` pins packs lifted by the semantic-register kernel (same five bases, the mutations re-expressed as row edits with rehash, §5.2's \"raw row edit\" rule) |\n76\t| `ledger_fixtures.cljc`, `ledger_fixture_test.cljc` (6), `ledger-v1.txt` | `park`/`halted` bodies derived from the checkpoint fixtures and offered/reported through `grant/offer!` and `completion/report!` | regenerate over packs (the projection digest and every frame change); file renamed `ledger-v2.txt` |\n77\t| `crash_cut_test.cljc` (2) | `lf/world`, `lf/park` bodies | follows the ledger fixtures |\n78\t| `authority/grant_test.cljc` (16), `completion_test.cljc` (21), `inherited_test.cljc` (18), `front_test.cljc` (29), `admission_test.cljc` (24), `input_test.cljc` (20), `reclaim_test.cljc` (21), `completion_fold_test.cljc` (8) | bodies through `checkpoint-fixtures`/`ledger-fixtures` where an offer, report or inherited id is admitted; otherwise ledger only | the fixture switch above; the inspector call in `grant/offer!`, `completion/report!`, `admission/accepted-baseline` becomes the rows inspector |\n79\t| `ledger_test` (14), `custody_test` (4), `durability_test` (10), `evidence_test` | ledger only | none |\n80\t\n81\t### 1.4 Phase 6 assets phase 7 builds on (no change expected)\n82\t\n83\t`rows_test.cljc` (31), `semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc` (27; rows 1-24 of §5.2 on three lanes, laws 1-6), `frame_test`, `layout_test`. The harness's `machine`, `load-program`, `project`/`source-index` and `canonical-under` are the templates for the new support namespace.\n84\t\n85\t---\n86\t\n87\t## 2. Old transport paths: deleted at phase 7 versus kept until phase 8\n88\t\n89\t**Deleted or replaced at phase 7** (their subject is replaced, so keeping them would test a dead path):\n90\t\n91\t- `holder.export/prepare`, `encode`, `freeze`, `rehydrate-fenced` as written over `handoff/export-task`, `inspect-recovery-body` and `rehydrate-fenced-body`: rewritten over rows. `enter` and `abort` survive unchanged.\n92\t- The export-recovery object version 1 (`:yin.k/export-recovery`, `:yin.k/version 1`, `:yin.k/snapshot-*`): replaced by version 2 (§3.2). No reader of version 1 remains.\n93\t- The driver's calls: `handoff/resume-task` in `lower` and `step-validating`; `checkpoint/inspect` in `step-validating`, `rebuilt-cell`, `reopen-holder`; `export/rehydrate-fenced` in `rebuilt-cell`; `:export-version` as a seam (compose's `holder-config-keys` and `driver-config` drop it). The `fork-step` keeps its shape over the rows lift.\n94\t- The authority's three inspector calls (`grant/offer!`, `completion/report!`, `admission/accepted-baseline`) switch to the rows inspector; the ledger's `:yin.k/baseline` shape gains `:yin.k/root` (§3.3).\n95\t- `lift-support`'s role as the D/E machine source: superseded by a semantic-register support namespace; `lift-support` itself stays for the old codec's tests.\n96\t- Tests deleted at 7: `driver_test/a-version-two-fork-lift-is-the-composition-choice-test`, `handoff_v2_holder_test/a-version-2-recovery-snapshot-freezes-and-rehydrates-fenced`, `export_test`'s rec1-specific rows (`complete-export-recovery-is-fenced-and-reproducible`, `module-store-dependencies-are-censused-to-a-fixed-point`, `version-one-recovery-retains-operation-ids-issues-and-local-enrollment`, `snapshot`-shaped assertions) are replaced by their rows equivalents in the same file.\n97\t\n98\t**Kept until phase 8, untouched** (the brief's rule: no new work on the old kernels and compilers):\n99\t\n100\t- `handoff.cljc` whole (version 0/1/2 lift, reader, `validate-body`, `rehydrate-fenced-body`, `inspect-recovery-body`; the last two become dead code with no caller).\n101\t- `checkpoint.cljc` (the version-1/2 inspector) and `checkpoint_test`/`checkpoint_fixtures`/`checkpoint-v1.txt`.\n102\t- `remote.cljc`'s version 0-2 pending lift/lower (`lift-frame`, `lower-frame`, `lift-pending`, `lower-pending`); rows uses only `lift-marker`/`lower-marker`/`cursor-profiles`.\n103\t- `lift_v1_test`, `handoff_test`, `handoff_v1_test`, every `handoff_v2_*` test but the one deftest above, `scalar_round_trip_test`, `v2_support`, `lift_support`, `handoff-v2.txt`, `scalars-v1.*`.\n104\t- `custody.cljc`, `ledger.cljc`, `authority.cljc`, `authority/*`, `holder/{driver,writer,reader,inbox,evidence}.cljc`, `compose.cljc`: not old transport; they change only at the call sites listed above.\n105\t\n106\t**Owner rulings honoured:** A is the only code identity (rows carry `{A vector}` and `:yin.k/trees`; the inspector names code by A only); the AST is never lost (`:source-index` is a lift input in every slice; the Stage E composition must supply it); cursor-cell sharing survives transport (the census, unchanged); BLAKE3 (row ids, pack address, intent digest, recovery address all under Jing's default); no backward compatibility (no version dispatch in the driver, fixtures regenerated, files renamed); native lanes excluded from Stage E.\n107\t\n108\t---\n109\t\n110\t## 3. Design questions phase 7 forces, with recommendation, cost, and who decides\n111\t\n112\t### 3.1 Exclusive and holder custody on rows: which rows carry lease, fence and holder state\n113\t\n114\t**Finding.** The root row's slot 16 `custody` is plain data (draft §5.5 table). UCF 7.2.1 is operative for version 3: \"no body carries an epoch; the epoch is learned only from the grant binding\". The holder state the driver needs at run time (`:yin.k/custody` on the machine: occurrence, lease, holder, epoch, arbitration, next-op-seq, `:protection`, `:input {:next :prefix}`, `:tenure`) is built by the lower from the **grant evidence**, never from the body (`accept-grant!` today). The fence (op-ids) already has slots: `:put [stream V op-id]`, `:ffi-request [... op-id]`, `:link-request [... envelope op-id]`.\n115\t\n116\t**Recommendation (Architect decides).**\n117\t- The root `custody` slot carries exactly the draft's table for `:blocked`/`:parked` exclusive roots: `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/arbitration {...} :yin.k/next-op-seq n}` plus `:yin.k/origin` on a successor; a halted exclusive root carries `{:yin.k/origin ...}` only; children nil (as now). Lease, epoch, holder, enrolled set, protection classes: **never** in a row.\n118\t- `lift-task` gains a `:header` option (the same header `holder.export/prepare` takes today). With a header it writes the slot by kind, refuses `:op-seq-exhausted` at 2^52-1 on a blocked or parked root, `:unprotected-pending` for a retained `:put`/`:ffi-request`/`:link-request` on an enrolled target without an op-id (root or child), and `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream for an op-id on an unenrolled target; a halted root with a header but no origin is refused (not downgraded). These are the version-1 rules, moved (`handoff.cljc:623-657`, `:1513-1523`).\n119\t- `lower-task` replaces `check-custody!` (nil only) by the version-1 custody stage: fork root under `:exclusive` → `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; exclusive root of kind `:blocked`/`:parked` requires `:grant` + `:protection` opts and runs the seven grant checks of `accept-grant!` (`handoff.cljc:3343-3420`, ported verbatim, keyed off the slot instead of body keys); every marker's protection class must match enrollment in both directions; op-id presence must equal enrollment per retained write; the restored machine gets `:yin.k/custody` from the evidence and `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from the slot **exactly** (no max); a halted exclusive root lowers with no grant, gated `:ended`. A `:fenced` option (recovery, §3.2) skips the grant and gates `:exporting`.\n120\t- The custody stage keeps its place: all four stages over the whole tree before the first `attach!` (continuation §7.2, codex F1/F2/F4/F7).\n121\t\n122\t**Fork spelling (OWNER decides, §3.10 Q1).** Phase 6 writes `custody nil` for a fork root; the draft's table spells `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` on blocked/parked fork roots and nil on halted ones. Both decide the policy from the root alone. Nil is landed and every pinned root id in `rows_test` and the harness depends on it; the explicit spelling costs about half a day of pin regeneration and makes the slot's reader one `case` on `:yin.k/policy`. I recommend **keeping nil** and amending the draft's table (fork = nil in every row), on the minimal-diff principle; the owner may prefer the draft's spelling.\n123\t\n124\t**Cost.** 1.5 days in 7a (lift option, three refusals, the ported grant checks, the protection stage, tests on three hosts).\n125\t\n126\t### 3.2 The export-recovery object re-based on rows\n127\t\n128\t**Finding.** Version 1 (`holder.export/freeze`) stores two bodies: the published body and a \"snapshot\" lifted with `::recovery true` (full census), because version-0 bodies omitted transitive module stores and the recovery must rehydrate everything. It also stores `:yin.k/header`, `:yin.k/descriptors` (the served set) and `:yin.k/state` (`gate`, `origins`, `issues` per wait, `issued`, children recursively). `rehydrate-fenced` restores the snapshot with `rehydrate-fenced-body` (gate `:exporting`, waits in `:yin.k/recovery-waits`, parked table emptied into the record, `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids` mapping resource ids back to carrier cursor cells), re-prepares from the retained served table and proves the published bytes are reproduced.\n129\t\n130\t**Recommendation (Architect decides).** Version 2:\n131\t\n132\t```clojure\n133\t{:yin.k/export-recovery true\n134\t :yin.k/version         2\n135\t :yin.k/pack-address    a        ; the pack's own address (§3.3)\n136\t :yin.k/root            B        ; the root id inside it\n137\t :yin.k/pack-bytes      base64\n138\t :yin.k/header          h        ; nil for a fork\n139\t :yin.k/descriptors     #{...}   ; the served set, as today\n140\t :yin.k/state           {:gate g :origins n :issued n :issues [..] :children {m state}}}\n141\t```\n142\t\n143\tOne body: the pack already carries the whole root store, every parked record and the module stores by reachability (continuation §2.5), so the \"snapshot extends body\" check and the fixed-point repair disappear. `rehydrate-fenced` = decode, verify `pack-address` and `root`, `lower-task` with `{:fenced true :header h}` (no grant, gate `:exporting`, waits moved to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`, parked records moved into the record, `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids` from the census), then `prepare` from the retained served table and `encode` must reproduce `pack-bytes` exactly. Engine-local state that is not canonical content (`issues`, `issued`, `origins`, `gate`) stays in `:yin.k/state` as today; `:yin.k/issue` on lowered `:put` waits is assigned sequentially in carried order (what `resume-task*` does, `handoff.cljc:3643-3650`) and overwritten from `:issues` on rehydration. The recovery address stays the BLAKE3 of the recovery bytes.\n144\t\n145\t**Cost.** 1.5 days in 7b (export.cljc rewrite, `:fenced` lower mode, export_test re-based).\n146\t\n147\t### 3.3 Checkpoint and ledger embedding of a row body root\n148\t\n149\t**Finding.** The authority stores the body bytes under `:yin.k/id` with `(:put-bytes-fn store)`, and both `dao.jing.mem` and `dao.jing.file` **refuse a put whose bytes do not hash to the address** (`mem.cljc:38`, `file.cljc:351`). The pack `{:yin.k/handoff true :yin.k/version 3 :yin.k/root B :yin.k/rows [...]}` does not hash to `B`. Further, `emit` builds `:yin.k/rows` as `(vec (vals rows))` of a hash map, so the pack's byte order is host-dependent today; `B` is not.\n150\t\n151\t**Recommendation (Architect decides; the draft already allows it, §10 answer 2: \"when a pack is addressed it gets its own address, distinct from the semantic root id\").**\n152\t- `emit` sorts `:yin.k/rows` by row id (bytewise). One root → one pack on every host. Root ids do not change, so no pin moves.\n153\t- Two identities, each used where it belongs: the **pack address** `a = BLAKE3(pack bytes)` is the storage key and the ledger's `:yin.k/id` (a snapshot variant is an encoding, as today); the **root id** `B` is the semantic identity and enters the baseline as `:yin.k/root`. Two packs with one root are variants of equal baseline (impossible after sorting, but harmless). The offer request, the fence record and the report's `:yin.k/result` carry `a`; `offer-decision`'s `(jing/segment-address? (:yin.k/id fact))` holds for `a`.\n154\t- The **rows inspector** (`yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint/inspect address bytes`): address matches bytes (`jing/segment-bytes-match?`), bytes decode through `rows/decode` (every row hash, canonical numbering, frames), the root's custody slot by kind (the version-1 header rules of `checkpoint.cljc:183-232` on the slot's map; children nil), every op-id on `:put`/`:ffi-request`/`:link-request` pendings in root and children against the root's occurrence, origin and counter (`check-op-id!` verbatim), duplicate ids refused, and the baseline `{:yin.k/kind :yin.k/root :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/origin :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq :yin.k/ops {op-id digest}}` where the intent digest is `BLAKE3(cbor [:yin.k/append target-identity payload-slot])` with the payload **as the wire slot holds it** (`[:lit x]` or `[:row id]`, both canonical). A fork pack answers `:yin.k/fork true` as `inspect-v2` does. Every refusal is data; nothing throws (as today).\n155\t- `admission/accepted-baseline` and `completion/successor-defect` read `:yin.k/next-op-seq` and `:yin.k/origin` from the baseline, unchanged. `ledger/offer-defect` is unchanged (`:yin.k/kind` ∈ `#{:blocked :parked}`, the baseline compared whole).\n156\t- Halted exit: the report's `:yin.k/result` is the halted pack's address; the terminal edge names it.\n157\t\n158\t**Cost.** 1.5 days in 7a (inspector, sorted emit, fixtures regenerated, the three authority call sites, `ledger-v2.txt`).\n159\t\n160\t### 3.4 Crash cuts: which cuts are meaningful when the body is a Merkle row set\n161\t\n162\t**Finding.** The named cuts (`stage_e_world/cuts`) cut the progress journal around mint, serve, store intent/ack, two content-store puts (recovery first, body second), fence, offer; the authority's commit; the reply journal; and composition ticks. They are meaningful at the granularity of **one store put per object**.\n163\t\n164\t**Recommendation (Architect decides).** Store the pack as **one** content-store object. Then every cut keeps its meaning: `:export/store-put-*` cuts the recovery v2 put, `:export/body-put-*` the pack put, and a pack is either whole or absent; a torn pack is impossible by the store's atomic insert. Storing rows individually (one object per row, Merkle-addressed) would make partial checkpoints possible and require a new cut family (`:pack/missing-row-before-fence`) and a new refusal path (`rows/decode` `:missing-row` at the authority), with no benefit in phase 7. Row-level storage is a phase-8+ option for deduplication across checkpoints and is out of scope. One new cut is worth adding cheaply: `:export/pack-put-after-recovery-missing` is already covered by `store-put-before` (recovery absent, nothing fenced); no addition needed.\n165\t\n166\t**Cost.** None beyond keeping the two-put order in `export-fence-step`.\n167\t\n168\t### 3.5 Publication of successors\n169\t\n170\t**Finding.** The protocol (`exit-mint` → prepare → freeze → fence → report → release → closure; the successor offer refused `:awaiting-completion` then admitted) is body-agnostic except for three values: the successor header (built from the machine's `:yin.k/custody`), the report's result address and the stored bytes.\n171\t\n172\t**Recommendation (decided).** No protocol change. The successor's header takes `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from the machine's custody map as today; the lift writes it into the slot; the inspector's `:counter-regression` check compares slot against baseline. The report carries the successor's pack address; `reopen-exit` rebuilds from the journaled mint and the fence as today. The one wrinkle: `reopen-holder` and `rebuilt-cell` read the kind and occurrence from the stored bytes through the inspector; the rows inspector answers the same keys.\n173\t\n174\t**Cost.** Included in 7b.\n175\t\n176\t### 3.6 Authority and capability tokens versus sealed refs on rows\n177\t\n178\t**Finding.** Rows re-seal every stream and cursor-ref under the receiver's owner on lower (`engine/issue-ref`), authenticate refs on lift, and carry no credential (UCF 7.7.8: \"the envelope carries no credential; the holder is authenticated by the attribution the composition supplies\"). The owner ruled (2026-10-12) that ShiBi tokens are a configurable policy at the `:admit` seam, and (2026-09-27) that ShiBi is a tuple space emerging from index and query interpreters, with `dao.stream.remote` offering only a capability-agnostic seam.\n179\t\n180\t**Recommendation (Architect decides; owner to confirm scope).** Phase 7 carries **no** capability token in any row and adds no row kind for one. Authority is ledger facts, attribution is the composition's resolver, tenure is lease evidence; sealed refs are the receiver's and are minted on lower. A token-bearing admission is ShiBi work at the admission seam, after phase 8.\n181\t\n182\t**Cost.** None.\n183\t\n184\t### 3.7 `:resume-mode` under version 2\n185\t\n186\t**Finding.** The owner's ruling (orchestrator log, 2026-10-11) is that the version-2 stack lane stays frozen and does not carry `:resume-mode`. Rows carry `:act` and `:tail` rows; a `:tail` is the register kernel's `:return-result` delivery and an `:act` the `:write-result` one (continuation §4.2, §4.3), so the information `:resume-mode` encodes is structural in version 3.\n187\t\n188\t**Recommendation (decided).** No work. Phase 7 does not touch version 2; version 3 needs no such key.\n189\t\n190\t### 3.8 Two gaps the brief did not list but phase 7 meets\n191\t\n192\t- **Continuation §11 Q4 (op-id rules on lower).** Closed by the rows inspector running inside `lower-task`'s custody stage for exclusive roots (below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent without origin, never the body's own occurrence). Fork roots refuse any op-id (`:fork-op-id`), as `inspect-v2` does. Decided, in 7a.\n193\t- **Continuation §11 Q3 (install verification).** `check-install!` verifies status, manifest name, exports and that the image loads, not link-id = parent nor image address = the manifest's. Adding both is about a quarter day in 7b. **OWNER decides** (§3.10 Q3); recommend yes, because the recovery object now trusts the pack for install children.\n194\t- **Continuation §11 Q1/Q10 (the `:running` wire phase).** Unchanged by phase 7; Stage E's installer scenarios use phase `:parked` only. Leave open.\n195\t\n196\t### 3.9 Which kernels take part\n197\t\n198\tStage E runs the **semantic-register kernel only** (the reference lane). The de Bruijn kernels are covered by the cross-engine laws of the harness (61/350, accepted) and are not Stage E holders or sources; the holder driver never lowers through a layout. This follows the owner's matrix ruling and avoids three times the process matrix.\n199\t\n200\t### 3.10 The owner's questions (blocking)\n201\t\n202\t- **Q1, fork spelling in the custody slot.** nil (landed; recommended) or the draft's `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` (half a day of pin regeneration; the draft is amended either way).\n203\t- **Q2, the REPL custody gap.** `yin.repl` composes the **old** `yin.vm.semantic` kernel (`repl.cljc:144`) and wires `compose` (`repl/main.cljc:979-1011`). After phase 7 the driver reads only packs, so the REPL's custody path cannot lift its machine until phase 8 renames the semantic-register kernel into `yin.vm.semantic`. Options: (a) accept the gap until 8 (recommended: `the-plain-composition-loads-and-drives-with-no-yin-repl-test` is re-based on a semantic-register receiver, and the REPL's `:custody` branch is marked inert until 8); (b) pull the REPL's kernel switch into phase 7 (an extra slice, two to three days, touching `yin.repl` and its tests, which 4b/5 did not plan).\n204\t- **Q3, the install checks of continuation §11 Q3** in 7b (recommended yes, a quarter day).\n205\t\n206\t---\n207\t\n208\t## 4. Slicing\n209\t\n210\tEach slice: files, dependencies, acceptance tests, hosts, days (opus implementation + review rounds at the observed 2-4 codex rounds per slice, about half a day per round including fixes).\n211\t\n212\t### 7a: custody on rows, the rows inspector, the authority's fixtures\n213\t\n214\t**Create.** `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows/checkpoint.cljc` (the inspector of §3.3); `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc` (semantic-register machine builders mirroring `lift_support`: `new-machine` over `sr/create-vm` with `tu/make-stream`, deterministic one-slot streams, `parked-reader`, `parked-writer`, `parked-explicit`, `parked-installer`, `parked-linker`, `halted-machine`, `halted-with-cursors`, `parked-module-store-cursors`, `with-op-id`, `serve-table`, `header`, `source-index` capture through `linearize/project`); `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc` + `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` (the five bases lifted by `rows/lift-task` under a header, the mutations as raw row edits with rehash where they touch a row and as root-slot edits otherwise, three anchors with full pack bytes); `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_test.cljc`; `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` (the lift and lower custody rules of §3.1: header by kind, the three lift refusals, the seven grant checks with zero attachments, protection in both directions, exact counter restore, halted exclusive lowers ended, fork under `:exclusive` refused).\n215\t\n216\t**Change.** `rows.cljc` (`emit` sorts rows by id; `lift-task` `:header`; `lower-task` custody stage with `:grant`/`:protection`/`:exclusive`/`:fenced` opts; `check-custody!` replaced); `authority/grant.cljc`, `authority/completion.cljc`, `authority/admission.cljc` (inspector call); `ledger.cljc` (none expected; `:yin.k/root` in the baseline is opaque to the fold); `ledger_fixtures.cljc` (`park`/`halted` from rows packs; `path` → `ledger-v2.txt`), `ledger_fixture_test`, `crash_cut_test`, `grant_test`, `completion_test`, `inherited_test`, `front_test` (fixture bodies only).\n217\t\n218\t**Depends on.** Nothing; owner's Q1.\n219\t\n220\t**Acceptance.** `rows_checkpoint_test` (every base accepted with its baseline; every mutation refused at its path; the file equals `render` on three hosts), `rows_custody_test`, `ledger_fixture_test` (every host rebuilds every frame byte for byte; the projection digest pinned), `crash_cut_test`, the eight authority tests green, `rows_test` and the safepoint harness unchanged (no root id moves), `store_write_audit_test` allowlist updated if the row table changes.\n221\t\n222\t**Hosts.** JVM, Node, Dart.\n223\t\n224\t**Days.** 2.5 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) = **4**.\n225\t\n226\t### 7b: the holder's export record, recovery v2, the driver's lower\n227\t\n228\t**Change.** `holder/export.cljc` (`prepare`/`encode` over `rows/lift-task` with `:header` and a `:serve-keyed` adaptor; `freeze` → recovery v2; `rehydrate-fenced` over `lower-task {:fenced true}`); `holder/driver.cljc` (`lower`, `step-validating`, `rebuilt-cell`, `reopen-holder`, `reopen-exit`, `source-mint`/`exit-mint` unchanged in shape; `:export-version` removed); `compose.cljc` (`holder-config-keys`/`driver-config` drop `:export-version`); `holder/export_test`, `holder/driver_test`, `holder/writer_test`, `holder/reader_test` (machines from `rows_support`; rec1 rows replaced; the v2 fork-lift deftest deleted); `handoff_v2_holder_test` (one deftest deleted).\n229\t\n230\t**Depends on.** 7a; owner's Q3 for the install checks (a quarter day inside this slice).\n231\t\n232\t**Acceptance.** `export_test` (recovery v2 freezes and rehydrates fenced with zero program IO, the published pack reproduced byte for byte, aliases and distinct cursor cells preserved, install children rehydrated without initialization or IO, a restarted abort refused), `driver_test` whole (117 deftests), `writer_test`, `reader_test`, `evidence_test`; the law \"zero attachments before the custody stage refuses\" pinned by attach counts.\n233\t\n234\t**Hosts.** JVM, Node, Dart.\n235\t\n236\t**Days.** 3 implementation + 2 review (4 rounds; the driver is 2.9k lines and its tests 4.3k) = **5**.\n237\t\n238\t### 7c: the composition and the compose and fencing rows\n239\t\n240\t**Change.** `fencing_rows_test` (rows lift under header, `lower-task` with grant opts), `compose_test` (semantic-register `:receiver`/`:machine`; the plain-composition test per Q2), `compose_rows_test` (41 deftests; `offer-variant`-style helpers re-emit packs; content-store cuts see recovery v2 then the pack); `yin.repl/main.cljc` only if Q2 answers (b).\n241\t\n242\t**Depends on.** 7b; owner's Q2.\n243\t\n244\t**Acceptance.** All three files green on three hosts; the slow deftest `completion-recovery-unknown-policy-transaction-is-reconciled-once-test` under `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1` on each lane.\n245\t\n246\t**Days.** 1.5 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) = **3**.\n247\t\n248\t### 7d: Stage E on the semantic-register kernel, three hosts, four process pairs\n249\t\n250\t**Change.** `stage_e_world.cljc` (`parked-reader-over`, `reading-writer` over `rows_support`; `holder-seams` supplies `:source-index`; `offer-variant!` re-emits; `snapshot`'s `:fences` reads pack digests), `stage_e_scenarios.cljc` (checks that name `:yin.k/id-counter` or body keys adjusted; row 1's \"two encodings\" assertion keeps its meaning), `stage_e_peer.cljc` (none expected), `stage_e_process_test.clj` (none expected; the Node peer build picks up the new namespaces), `stage_e_rows_test.cljc` (none expected).\n251\t\n252\t**Depends on.** 7c (the world port can begin on 7b and run in-process while 7c is in review).\n253\t\n254\t**Acceptance.** `stage_e_rows_test` on JVM, Node and Dart (slow tier, `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`); `stage_e_process_test` on the JVM over `[[:jvm :jvm] [:jvm :node] [:node :jvm] [:node :node]]`, every life its own process, every cut a SIGKILL, `target/stage-e-durability.edn` appended; the fast-lane gate `the-cross-host-handoff-across-processes-test` first. Row 10's canonical-byte agreement across hosts now holds for packs as well (sorted rows).\n255\t\n256\t**Hosts.** JVM, Node, Dart (in-process); JVM↔Node (processes).\n257\t\n258\t**Days.** 2 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) + 0.5 for the slow matrix runs (the process matrix is about an hour per full pass on the remote hosts, per build-n-test) = **4**.\n259\t\n260\t### 7e: documentation\n261\t\n262\t**Change.** `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §2.5, §2.6 (custody slot by kind), §7.1-7.2 (the `:header` lift, the custody and fenced lower stages), §8.4 (phase 7 met), §10 (a 10.7 inventory), §11 (Q3, Q4 closed; Q1/Q10 left); `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §5.5 (the custody table as ruled), §7 (pack address versus root id, sorted rows), §9 (the fenced lower); `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` 7.7.4 (recovery v2 paragraph replacing the version-1 one), 7.11.1 clauses 1-5 and 10 (stage letters updated); `docs/orchestrator-log.md` entry.\n263\t\n264\t**Depends on.** The owner's answers; concurrent with 7c/7d.\n265\t\n266\t**Days.** 1 + 1 review round = **1.5**.\n267\t\n268\t---\n269\t\n270\t## 5. Risks and what could force a redesign\n271\t\n272\t1. **The identity split (pack address versus root id).** If the owner or a reviewer insists the ledger's `:yin.k/id` be the root id, the content store contract must change (a verifier-taking put) or the authority must store under the pack address and record both; the second is what §3.3 recommends anyway, so the redesign cost is in naming, not mechanics. Sorting rows removes the host-order variance that would otherwise make JVM and Node packs distinct variants.\n273\t2. **Fenced rehydration on the semantic-register kernel.** `rehydrate-fenced-body` relies on the version-0/1/2 lower's `:fenced?` arm (`handoff.cljc:3688-3699`, `:3722-3725`): gate `:exporting`, waits parked in `:yin.k/recovery-waits`, `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids`, the FFI pair resources for `:ffi-request` pendings, `id-counter` carried exactly. `lower-task` has none of this; porting it is routine but the install-child case (children exporting, their parked records moved to the child's record) is exercised only by `export_test`. Medium.\n274\t3. **Install children on the semantic-register kernel under the holder.** The harness proves rows 7-12 (4b stage 2) for lift/lower; the holder's reader (held observations, replay through child call ids) over semantic-register install children has no test today. If `reader.cljc`'s walk assumes a field the new kernel spells differently, 7b finds it. Low-medium: both kernels share `engine.cljc` for `:installs`, `:wait-set`, `:yin.k/held`.\n275\t4. **The REPL gap (Q2).** If the owner wants the REPL's custody demo live between 7 and 8, phase 7 grows by a slice and touches the 4b/5 \"no REPL edit\" boundary.\n276\t5. **Stage E's pinned row-5 behaviour** (\"the exposed gap marker is the first and only commit\", pending an Architect ruling) is inherited unchanged; a ruling during phase 7 would change `row5`'s check, not the transport.\n277\t6. **Dart.** Stage E runs in-process only on Dart (as today); the numbering budget (`default-budget`) is far above Stage E's task sizes. The known Dart traps (reader whitespace, `for` over chunked seqs, multi-key `assoc` on nil, duplicate protocol params) apply to the new support namespace; the harness already avoids them.\n278\t7. **Review load.** Phase 6b took three codex rounds on a 1.8k-line file; 7b touches the 2.9k-line driver through eight call sites plus `export.cljc` whole. Budgeted at four rounds.\n279\t8. **What would force a redesign of rows:** a requirement that the body's identity be a byte address (contradicts Merkle rows; §3.3 resolves it with two identities); a requirement to carry lease or epoch in the body (contradicts UCF 7.2.1, not proposed); row-level storage with partial checkpoints (deferred, §3.4). None is live.\n280\t\n281\t---\n282\t\n283\t## 6. Order of work and concurrency\n284\t\n285\t```\n286\t7a (custody, inspector, fixtures)        ──────────┐\n287\t                                                   ▼\n288\t7b (export record, recovery v2, driver)  ──────────┐\n289\t                                                   ▼\n290\t7c (compose, compose/fencing rows)       ──────┐   ├── 7d world port may start here\n291\t                                               ▼   ▼\n292\t7d (Stage E, in-process three hosts, then the process matrix)\n293\t7e (docs) ─ starts after the owner's answers, lands beside 7c/7d\n294\t```\n295\t\n296\t- 7a is the only slice that needs an owner answer before it starts (Q1). Q2 gates one deftest in 7c and possibly an extra slice; Q3 gates a quarter day in 7b.\n297\t- Concurrent work: 7e with 7c/7d; the 7d world port (`stage_e_world` over `rows_support`) with 7c's review, since it depends on 7b's `export`/`driver` only. The old-codec tests are never touched, so no conflict with other worktrees; `rows.cljc` is the one file 7a and the harness share, and 7a keeps every pinned root id.\n298\t- Landing cadence (owner's tiers): quick tier at every checkpoint; the slow tier (Stage E, the completion-recovery slow deftest, the process matrix) before each merge of 7c and 7d and once at the phase boundary; one lane set per machine, Dart in its own clone on the remote host.\n299\t\n300\t---\n301\t\n302\t## 7. Honest total\n303\t\n304\t| Slice | Implementation (opus) | Review rounds | Days |\n305\t|---|---|---|---|\n306\t| 7a | 2.5 | 3 | 4 |\n307\t| 7b | 3 | 4 | 5 |\n308\t| 7c | 1.5 | 3 | 3 |\n309\t| 7d | 2 | 3 (+0.5 matrix runs) | 4 |\n310\t| 7e | 1 | 1 | 1.5 |\n311\t| **Total** | **10** | | **17.5 sequential** |\n312\t\n313\tWith 7e concurrent and the 7d port overlapping 7c, the critical path is 7a → 7b → 7c → 7d: about **14 days** of work; **12 days median** if reviews average the lower end (two rounds on 7a/7c), **17 at the worst** (four rounds everywhere, one redesign loop on the identity split). Phase 6's three slices went from first commit to merge in three calendar days with the design already written and reviews back-to-back; phase 7 has four code slices over twice the surface, so calendar time of one to two weeks is realistic if codex capacity holds.\n314\t\n315\t---\n316\t\n317\t## Verdict\n318\t\n319\t**NOT READY.** Blocking questions for the owner: **Q1** fork spelling in the custody slot (nil, recommended, or the draft's explicit map); **Q2** the REPL custody gap until phase 8 (accept, recommended, or pull the REPL kernel switch into phase 7); **Q3** the install link-id and image-address checks in 7b (recommended yes). Every other design point above is decided by the Architect within the owner's rulings and is ready for an implementation brief for 7a on receipt of Q1.\n320\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"108e1cb8-f707-457b-be14-6feb1e135c49","uuid":"0e97a37e-3b4e-482a-8d7f-19d79c561103","timestamp":"2026-10-13T10:02:40.983Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7b/collab/phase7-plan.md","content":"Created-Local: 2026-10-13 16:10 +07\n# Phase 7 plan: Stage D/E rerun over the row carrier (Architect, plan only)\n\nAuthor: claude-fable-5-1. Worktree `srvm-phase7-plan` at `781a9ba8` (phases 1-6 merged). Read: the four design documents named in the brief, `rows.cljc`, `handoff.cljc` (export-task, the reader, resume-task*, the recovery paths), `holder/{export,driver,writer,reader}.cljc`, `checkpoint.cljc`, `custody.cljc`, `ledger.cljc`, `authority/{grant,completion,admission}.cljc`, `compose.cljc`, the Stage E world, scenarios, peer, process test and rows test, the checkpoint and ledger fixtures and tests, `lift_support.cljc`, `handoff_v2_holder_test.cljc`, the semantic-register safepoint harness, `dao.jing.{file,mem}` put contracts, `yin.repl`'s composition wiring. No code or doc was edited.\n\n## Summary for the owner (15 lines)\n\n1. Every Stage D/E test today runs on the **old** `yin.vm.semantic` kernel through `handoff.cljc` bodies (version 0 stream-CBOR, version 1 and 2 jing-CBOR); the row carrier (version 3) runs only on the semantic-register kernel and only with `custody nil`.\n2. Phase 7 is therefore one move, done in four slices: teach the row carrier custody, re-base the holder (export record, recovery object, driver lower) and the authority's inspector on row packs, then rerun the composition and the Stage E crash matrix on the semantic-register kernel, three hosts.\n3. The driver, writer, reader, compose, authority, ledger and front are **kernel-agnostic**: they read machine fields that `yin.vm.engine` owns (`:wait-set`, `:installs`, `:yin.k/gate`, `:yin.k/custody`). They change only where they call `handoff/*` or `checkpoint/inspect` (eight call sites).\n4. Nothing of version 0/1/2 is rewritten; it dies at phase 8. Phase 7 deletes only what it replaces: the version-1 export-recovery object, the holder's use of `export-task`/`resume-task`, the authority's use of the version-1 inspector. The old codec and its direct tests stay green and dead until phase 8.\n5. One hard fact drives the design: `dao.jing` content stores **verify bytes against the address on put**, and a row pack's bytes do not hash to its root id. So the checkpoint has two identities: the pack address (storage, the ledger's `:yin.k/id`) and the root id (semantic, inside the baseline). `emit` must sort its rows so one root gives one pack on every host.\n6. Custody travels as the root's `custody` slot exactly as the draft's §5.5 table says (policy, occurrence, arbitration, next-op-seq, origin); lease, epoch and holder never enter a body (UCF 7.2.1 stands); op-ids already have their slots on `:put`, `:ffi-request` and `:link-request` pendings.\n7. The recovery object loses its second \"snapshot\" body (rows already carry the whole store and every parked record) and keeps only holder-local engine state (gate, issues, issued, origins) beside the pack.\n8. Crash cuts stay meaningful unchanged if the pack is stored as **one** blob; storing rows individually would add a torn-pack cut family and is not recommended.\n9. `:resume-mode` stays out: the version-2 stack lane is frozen until phase 8, and rows carry `:act`/`:tail`, which subsume it. No work.\n10. Three questions need the owner: fork spelling in the custody slot (nil as landed, or the draft's explicit `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}`); the REPL custody gap (the REPL composes the old kernel until phase 8, so its custody path is dead between 7 and 8 unless the kernel switch is pulled forward); whether the Q3 install checks (link-id = parent, image address = manifest) land in 7b.\n11. Slices: 7a custody on rows and the rows inspector with regenerated fixtures; 7b holder export, recovery v2 and the driver's lower; 7c the composition and the compose/fencing row tests; 7d the Stage E world and matrix across processes; 7e docs. 7a → 7b → 7c → 7d, with 7e and the 7d world port overlapping 7c.\n12. Estimate: 12 working days median (9 implementation, the rest 2-4 codex rounds per slice and the slow-tier runs), 17 at the worst; phase 6's three slices landed in three calendar days with the design pre-written, so calendar could be shorter.\n13. Biggest risks: the identity split surprising the ledger's variant rules (mitigated by sorting rows); the fenced-wait rehydration of install children on the semantic-register kernel, exercised only by the harness today; Dart runs only the in-process Stage E form (as today).\n14. Nothing found that forces a redesign of rows or the ledger; the de Bruijn lanes are not Stage E participants (the 61/350 matrix covers only the cross-engine laws).\n15. Verdict at the end: **NOT READY** until the owner answers the three questions in §3.10; everything else is decided below and can start on 7a the moment the fork-spelling answer lands.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Inventory: every Stage D/E scenario and test, its transport today, what it needs on rows\n\nVocabulary (UCF 7.5.3): a *cursor* is the immutable `dao.stream` value; a *cursor cell* is the carrier's box (`:yin.k/cc-<n>` in rows, `:yin.k/c-<n>` in versions 0-2); a *cursor-ref* is the sealed reference a program holds; a *cell* is only the heap box.\n\nTransport codes: **v0** = `handoff/export-task` with a nil header, stream CBOR, fork; **v1** = the same with a header, jing CBOR, exclusive; **v2** = `{:version 2}`, the four-profile body (frozen); **rec1** = the export-recovery object version 1 (`holder.export/freeze`, embeds a v0/v1/v2 body plus a full-census snapshot); **ckpt** = `checkpoint/inspect` over v1/v2 bytes; **ledger** = `yin.vm.ucf.ledger` records (no body); **rows** = `rows/lift-task`/`lower-task`, version 3.\n\n### 1.1 Stage E (the acceptance matrix, `stage_e_*`)\n\nThe world (`stage_e_world.cljc`) builds both machines with `lift-support/new-machine`, which is `yin.vm.semantic/create-vm` (old kernel). Every life drives `compose` only; the export is v1 (`:export-version` nil), the recovery object rec1, the offer carries v1 bytes, the authority inspects with ckpt. Dart runs the in-process form only; JVM and Node also run the separate-process form over the four ordered pairs.\n\n| Scenario (ids) | Count | Parking shape (continuation §5.2 row) | Transport today | Needs on rows |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `[:fork]` | 1 | row 1 (blocked read, `:parked` world) | v0 lift, no bracket | rows lift with no header → root custody nil (or fork spelling, §3.1); the driver's `fork-step` answers pack bytes and address |\n| `[:row-7 cut]` × 5 cuts | 5 | row 1 at the source; successor is the same shape | v1 export, rec1, ckpt on offer and report | rows export under header; recovery v2; rows inspector on offer and report; successor pack address as `:yin.k/result` |\n| `[:row-7 :failure-lower]`, `[:row-7 :lost-authority]` | 2 | row 1 | v1, ckpt | as above; failure-lower is `attach!` returning not-found inside `lower-task` step 11 |\n| `[:row-4 cut]` × 3 | 3 | row 1 then row 2 (`:rows` world: read, write to the enrolled target, write to the side log, halt) | v1 with an op-id on the `:put`, ckpt baseline, admission | rows lift with the enrolled set (new `:unprotected-pending`/unenrolled checks); op-id slot already exists; baseline from the rows inspector |\n| `[:row-3 variant]` × 2 | 2 | rows 1, 2 | v1, admission | as row 4 |\n| `[:row-5 variant]` × 2 | 2 | rows 1, 2; evicted kept cursor (gap) | v1; input records | unchanged protocol; the kept cursor position is a cursor-cell body `[stream position]` in rows |\n| `[:row-2 cut]` × 14 cuts | 14 | row 1 | v1 export phases: mint, serve, store recovery (`store-put-*`), store body (`body-put-*`), fence, offer | the same phases over the pack; see §3.4 for why the cut set stays |\n| `[:row-2-carrier outcome]` × 4 | 4 | row 1 | v1 offer appends, abort | unchanged (`export/abort` is machine-level) |\n| `[:row-1]` | 1 | row 1; a second encoding minted by rewriting `:yin.k/id-counter` in the CBOR body | v1 variant bytes | the variant must be re-emitted: decode pack → change the root's id-counter slot → `emit` → new root id and new pack; equal baseline (§3.3) |\n| `[:row-6 ...]` × 3 | 3 | row 1 | v1; partition and lost reply | unchanged |\n| `[:clause-8 variant]` × 2, `[:clause-8 :closed]` | 3 | rows 1, 2; cross-target | v1, admission, inherited ids read the accepted checkpoint through ckpt | `accepted-baseline` must inspect the stored pack with the rows inspector |\n| `[:clause-3 :regrant]`, `[:clause-3 :carried-put]` | 2 | row 2 with an id assigned before export | v1 carries the id | rows carry `:op-id` on the `:put` pending slot; the successor's counter from the custody slot |\n| `[:journal cut]` × 7, `{:fail ...}` × 2 | 9 | row 1 | v1; inbox retention, journal brackets | unchanged except the fence record's two addresses are recovery v2 and the pack |\n\nTotal: 51 scenarios (`all-scenarios`), 12 `deftest`s in `stage_e_rows_test` (8 slow) and 12 in `stage_e_process_test` (all slow, JVM only, four host pairs, Node peer compiled by shadow with the peer as main).\n\n**What the world itself needs:** `parked-reader-over` and `reading-writer` built with `yin.vm.semantic-register/create-vm` and `sr/load-vector` over `linearize/project` (as the harness's `machine`/`load-program` do), a `:source-index` supplied to the lift (the composition's `{A #{tree}}`, continuation §6.1), `holder-seams` unchanged, and `offer-variant!` re-emitting a pack instead of patching CBOR.\n\n### 1.2 Stage D (the driver, the holder halves, the compose rows)\n\n| Test file (deftests) | Transport today | What it tests | Needs on rows |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `holder/driver_test.cljc` (118) | v0/v1/v2 through `handoff/resume-task`, `export/*`, rec1, ckpt | the whole driver: brackets, inbox, grant, lower, run, exit, reopen, abort, enrollment | machines from a semantic-register support namespace; the three driver call sites (`lower`, `step-validating`, `rebuilt-cell`/`reopen-holder`/`reopen-exit`) over rows; `a-version-two-fork-lift-is-the-composition-choice-test` deleted at 7 (its subject is the frozen v2 lane; nothing else exercises `:export-version 2` through the driver) |\n| `holder/export_test.cljc` (26) | v0/v1/v2 enter/prepare/encode/freeze/rehydrate/abort | the exporting state and recovery | `enter`, `abort`, hold refusals: unchanged (machine-level); prepare/encode/freeze/rehydrate: rewritten over rows and recovery v2; the \"snapshot extends body\" and \"transitive module store fixed point\" tests disappear (rows carry everything once) |\n| `holder/writer_test.cljc` (29) | machines with a hand-set `:yin.k/custody` map; no transport | assign, envelope, retention, discharge, closes | port the machine builders to the semantic-register kernel; logic unchanged (`writer.cljc` reads engine fields only) |\n| `holder/reader_test.cljc` (24) | same | held observations, replay, frontier | same |\n| `holder/evidence_test.cljc` (10) | ledger | binding evidence | none |\n| `fencing_rows_test.cljc` (4) | v1 lift + writer + reader + authority, no compose | rows 2, 4, 5 fencing halves | rows lift under header; `lower-task` with the grant opts |\n| `compose_test.cljc` (13) | v0/v1 through compose | open!, fork label, substrate checks, reopen, the plain composition | the `:receiver` and `:machine` seams become semantic-register machines; `the-plain-composition-loads-and-drives-with-no-yin-repl-test` reads the REPL's wiring (see §3.10 Q2) |\n| `compose_rows_test.cljc` (41, 1 slow) | v1 through compose; rec1; ckpt | rows 1-8 same host, completion recovery, journal cuts around freeze/storage/fencing, the content-store cuts | as compose; the content-store cut tests must see two puts (recovery v2, pack) as today |\n| `lift_v1_test.cljc` (29) | v1 `export-task` | header rules, enrolled refusals, canonical bytes, cursor cells | **kept until phase 8** (tests the old codec directly); its custody rules are re-pinned on rows in a new `rows_custody_test` (7a) |\n| `handoff_v2_custody_test.cljc` (5), `handoff_v2_holder_test.cljc` (6), the other `handoff_v2_*` (8 files), `handoff_test`, `handoff_v1_test`, `scalar_round_trip_test` | v0/v1/v2 codec | the old codec and layouts | kept until phase 8, except `a-version-2-recovery-snapshot-freezes-and-rehydrates-fenced` (tests rec1 freeze, deleted at 7) and `a-poisoned-receiver-behaves-as-a-clean-one` (goes through `resume-task` only, keep) |\n\n### 1.3 Stage C (the authority), where a body enters\n\n| Test file (deftests) | Body use today | Needs on rows |\n|---|---|---|\n| `checkpoint_fixtures.cljc`, `checkpoint_test.cljc` (14), `test/resources/.../checkpoint-v1.txt` | five bases lifted by the old semantic kernel through v1, 48 mutations, 3 byte anchors | **kept as the old inspector's test** until phase 8; a new `rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc` + `rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` pins packs lifted by the semantic-register kernel (same five bases, the mutations re-expressed as row edits with rehash, §5.2's \"raw row edit\" rule) |\n| `ledger_fixtures.cljc`, `ledger_fixture_test.cljc` (6), `ledger-v1.txt` | `park`/`halted` bodies derived from the checkpoint fixtures and offered/reported through `grant/offer!` and `completion/report!` | regenerate over packs (the projection digest and every frame change); file renamed `ledger-v2.txt` |\n| `crash_cut_test.cljc` (2) | `lf/world`, `lf/park` bodies | follows the ledger fixtures |\n| `authority/grant_test.cljc` (16), `completion_test.cljc` (21), `inherited_test.cljc` (18), `front_test.cljc` (29), `admission_test.cljc` (24), `input_test.cljc` (20), `reclaim_test.cljc` (21), `completion_fold_test.cljc` (8) | bodies through `checkpoint-fixtures`/`ledger-fixtures` where an offer, report or inherited id is admitted; otherwise ledger only | the fixture switch above; the inspector call in `grant/offer!`, `completion/report!`, `admission/accepted-baseline` becomes the rows inspector |\n| `ledger_test` (14), `custody_test` (4), `durability_test` (10), `evidence_test` | ledger only | none |\n\n### 1.4 Phase 6 assets phase 7 builds on (no change expected)\n\n`rows_test.cljc` (31), `semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc` (27; rows 1-24 of §5.2 on three lanes, laws 1-6), `frame_test`, `layout_test`. The harness's `machine`, `load-program`, `project`/`source-index` and `canonical-under` are the templates for the new support namespace.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Old transport paths: deleted at phase 7 versus kept until phase 8\n\n**Deleted or replaced at phase 7** (their subject is replaced, so keeping them would test a dead path):\n\n- `holder.export/prepare`, `encode`, `freeze`, `rehydrate-fenced` as written over `handoff/export-task`, `inspect-recovery-body` and `rehydrate-fenced-body`: rewritten over rows. `enter` and `abort` survive unchanged.\n- The export-recovery object version 1 (`:yin.k/export-recovery`, `:yin.k/version 1`, `:yin.k/snapshot-*`): replaced by version 2 (§3.2). No reader of version 1 remains.\n- The driver's calls: `handoff/resume-task` in `lower` and `step-validating`; `checkpoint/inspect` in `step-validating`, `rebuilt-cell`, `reopen-holder`; `export/rehydrate-fenced` in `rebuilt-cell`; `:export-version` as a seam (compose's `holder-config-keys` and `driver-config` drop it). The `fork-step` keeps its shape over the rows lift.\n- The authority's three inspector calls (`grant/offer!`, `completion/report!`, `admission/accepted-baseline`) switch to the rows inspector; the ledger's `:yin.k/baseline` shape gains `:yin.k/root` (§3.3).\n- `lift-support`'s role as the D/E machine source: superseded by a semantic-register support namespace; `lift-support` itself stays for the old codec's tests.\n- Tests deleted at 7: `driver_test/a-version-two-fork-lift-is-the-composition-choice-test`, `handoff_v2_holder_test/a-version-2-recovery-snapshot-freezes-and-rehydrates-fenced`, `export_test`'s rec1-specific rows (`complete-export-recovery-is-fenced-and-reproducible`, `module-store-dependencies-are-censused-to-a-fixed-point`, `version-one-recovery-retains-operation-ids-issues-and-local-enrollment`, `snapshot`-shaped assertions) are replaced by their rows equivalents in the same file.\n\n**Kept until phase 8, untouched** (the brief's rule: no new work on the old kernels and compilers):\n\n- `handoff.cljc` whole (version 0/1/2 lift, reader, `validate-body`, `rehydrate-fenced-body`, `inspect-recovery-body`; the last two become dead code with no caller).\n- `checkpoint.cljc` (the version-1/2 inspector) and `checkpoint_test`/`checkpoint_fixtures`/`checkpoint-v1.txt`.\n- `remote.cljc`'s version 0-2 pending lift/lower (`lift-frame`, `lower-frame`, `lift-pending`, `lower-pending`); rows uses only `lift-marker`/`lower-marker`/`cursor-profiles`.\n- `lift_v1_test`, `handoff_test`, `handoff_v1_test`, every `handoff_v2_*` test but the one deftest above, `scalar_round_trip_test`, `v2_support`, `lift_support`, `handoff-v2.txt`, `scalars-v1.*`.\n- `custody.cljc`, `ledger.cljc`, `authority.cljc`, `authority/*`, `holder/{driver,writer,reader,inbox,evidence}.cljc`, `compose.cljc`: not old transport; they change only at the call sites listed above.\n\n**Owner rulings honoured:** A is the only code identity (rows carry `{A vector}` and `:yin.k/trees`; the inspector names code by A only); the AST is never lost (`:source-index` is a lift input in every slice; the Stage E composition must supply it); cursor-cell sharing survives transport (the census, unchanged); BLAKE3 (row ids, pack address, intent digest, recovery address all under Jing's default); no backward compatibility (no version dispatch in the driver, fixtures regenerated, files renamed); native lanes excluded from Stage E.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Design questions phase 7 forces, with recommendation, cost, and who decides\n\n### 3.1 Exclusive and holder custody on rows: which rows carry lease, fence and holder state\n\n**Finding.** The root row's slot 16 `custody` is plain data (draft §5.5 table). UCF 7.2.1 is operative for version 3: \"no body carries an epoch; the epoch is learned only from the grant binding\". The holder state the driver needs at run time (`:yin.k/custody` on the machine: occurrence, lease, holder, epoch, arbitration, next-op-seq, `:protection`, `:input {:next :prefix}`, `:tenure`) is built by the lower from the **grant evidence**, never from the body (`accept-grant!` today). The fence (op-ids) already has slots: `:put [stream V op-id]`, `:ffi-request [... op-id]`, `:link-request [... envelope op-id]`.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides).**\n- The root `custody` slot carries exactly the draft's table for `:blocked`/`:parked` exclusive roots: `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/arbitration {...} :yin.k/next-op-seq n}` plus `:yin.k/origin` on a successor; a halted exclusive root carries `{:yin.k/origin ...}` only; children nil (as now). Lease, epoch, holder, enrolled set, protection classes: **never** in a row.\n- `lift-task` gains a `:header` option (the same header `holder.export/prepare` takes today). With a header it writes the slot by kind, refuses `:op-seq-exhausted` at 2^52-1 on a blocked or parked root, `:unprotected-pending` for a retained `:put`/`:ffi-request`/`:link-request` on an enrolled target without an op-id (root or child), and `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream for an op-id on an unenrolled target; a halted root with a header but no origin is refused (not downgraded). These are the version-1 rules, moved (`handoff.cljc:623-657`, `:1513-1523`).\n- `lower-task` replaces `check-custody!` (nil only) by the version-1 custody stage: fork root under `:exclusive` → `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; exclusive root of kind `:blocked`/`:parked` requires `:grant` + `:protection` opts and runs the seven grant checks of `accept-grant!` (`handoff.cljc:3343-3420`, ported verbatim, keyed off the slot instead of body keys); every marker's protection class must match enrollment in both directions; op-id presence must equal enrollment per retained write; the restored machine gets `:yin.k/custody` from the evidence and `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from the slot **exactly** (no max); a halted exclusive root lowers with no grant, gated `:ended`. A `:fenced` option (recovery, §3.2) skips the grant and gates `:exporting`.\n- The custody stage keeps its place: all four stages over the whole tree before the first `attach!` (continuation §7.2, codex F1/F2/F4/F7).\n\n**Fork spelling (OWNER decides, §3.10 Q1).** Phase 6 writes `custody nil` for a fork root; the draft's table spells `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` on blocked/parked fork roots and nil on halted ones. Both decide the policy from the root alone. Nil is landed and every pinned root id in `rows_test` and the harness depends on it; the explicit spelling costs about half a day of pin regeneration and makes the slot's reader one `case` on `:yin.k/policy`. I recommend **keeping nil** and amending the draft's table (fork = nil in every row), on the minimal-diff principle; the owner may prefer the draft's spelling.\n\n**Cost.** 1.5 days in 7a (lift option, three refusals, the ported grant checks, the protection stage, tests on three hosts).\n\n### 3.2 The export-recovery object re-based on rows\n\n**Finding.** Version 1 (`holder.export/freeze`) stores two bodies: the published body and a \"snapshot\" lifted with `::recovery true` (full census), because version-0 bodies omitted transitive module stores and the recovery must rehydrate everything. It also stores `:yin.k/header`, `:yin.k/descriptors` (the served set) and `:yin.k/state` (`gate`, `origins`, `issues` per wait, `issued`, children recursively). `rehydrate-fenced` restores the snapshot with `rehydrate-fenced-body` (gate `:exporting`, waits in `:yin.k/recovery-waits`, parked table emptied into the record, `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids` mapping resource ids back to carrier cursor cells), re-prepares from the retained served table and proves the published bytes are reproduced.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides).** Version 2:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/export-recovery true\n :yin.k/version         2\n :yin.k/pack-address    a        ; the pack's own address (§3.3)\n :yin.k/root            B        ; the root id inside it\n :yin.k/pack-bytes      base64\n :yin.k/header          h        ; nil for a fork\n :yin.k/descriptors     #{...}   ; the served set, as today\n :yin.k/state           {:gate g :origins n :issued n :issues [..] :children {m state}}}\n```\n\nOne body: the pack already carries the whole root store, every parked record and the module stores by reachability (continuation §2.5), so the \"snapshot extends body\" check and the fixed-point repair disappear. `rehydrate-fenced` = decode, verify `pack-address` and `root`, `lower-task` with `{:fenced true :header h}` (no grant, gate `:exporting`, waits moved to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`, parked records moved into the record, `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids` from the census), then `prepare` from the retained served table and `encode` must reproduce `pack-bytes` exactly. Engine-local state that is not canonical content (`issues`, `issued`, `origins`, `gate`) stays in `:yin.k/state` as today; `:yin.k/issue` on lowered `:put` waits is assigned sequentially in carried order (what `resume-task*` does, `handoff.cljc:3643-3650`) and overwritten from `:issues` on rehydration. The recovery address stays the BLAKE3 of the recovery bytes.\n\n**Cost.** 1.5 days in 7b (export.cljc rewrite, `:fenced` lower mode, export_test re-based).\n\n### 3.3 Checkpoint and ledger embedding of a row body root\n\n**Finding.** The authority stores the body bytes under `:yin.k/id` with `(:put-bytes-fn store)`, and both `dao.jing.mem` and `dao.jing.file` **refuse a put whose bytes do not hash to the address** (`mem.cljc:38`, `file.cljc:351`). The pack `{:yin.k/handoff true :yin.k/version 3 :yin.k/root B :yin.k/rows [...]}` does not hash to `B`. Further, `emit` builds `:yin.k/rows` as `(vec (vals rows))` of a hash map, so the pack's byte order is host-dependent today; `B` is not.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides; the draft already allows it, §10 answer 2: \"when a pack is addressed it gets its own address, distinct from the semantic root id\").**\n- `emit` sorts `:yin.k/rows` by row id (bytewise). One root → one pack on every host. Root ids do not change, so no pin moves.\n- Two identities, each used where it belongs: the **pack address** `a = BLAKE3(pack bytes)` is the storage key and the ledger's `:yin.k/id` (a snapshot variant is an encoding, as today); the **root id** `B` is the semantic identity and enters the baseline as `:yin.k/root`. Two packs with one root are variants of equal baseline (impossible after sorting, but harmless). The offer request, the fence record and the report's `:yin.k/result` carry `a`; `offer-decision`'s `(jing/segment-address? (:yin.k/id fact))` holds for `a`.\n- The **rows inspector** (`yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint/inspect address bytes`): address matches bytes (`jing/segment-bytes-match?`), bytes decode through `rows/decode` (every row hash, canonical numbering, frames), the root's custody slot by kind (the version-1 header rules of `checkpoint.cljc:183-232` on the slot's map; children nil), every op-id on `:put`/`:ffi-request`/`:link-request` pendings in root and children against the root's occurrence, origin and counter (`check-op-id!` verbatim), duplicate ids refused, and the baseline `{:yin.k/kind :yin.k/root :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/origin :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq :yin.k/ops {op-id digest}}` where the intent digest is `BLAKE3(cbor [:yin.k/append target-identity payload-slot])` with the payload **as the wire slot holds it** (`[:lit x]` or `[:row id]`, both canonical). A fork pack answers `:yin.k/fork true` as `inspect-v2` does. Every refusal is data; nothing throws (as today).\n- `admission/accepted-baseline` and `completion/successor-defect` read `:yin.k/next-op-seq` and `:yin.k/origin` from the baseline, unchanged. `ledger/offer-defect` is unchanged (`:yin.k/kind` ∈ `#{:blocked :parked}`, the baseline compared whole).\n- Halted exit: the report's `:yin.k/result` is the halted pack's address; the terminal edge names it.\n\n**Cost.** 1.5 days in 7a (inspector, sorted emit, fixtures regenerated, the three authority call sites, `ledger-v2.txt`).\n\n### 3.4 Crash cuts: which cuts are meaningful when the body is a Merkle row set\n\n**Finding.** The named cuts (`stage_e_world/cuts`) cut the progress journal around mint, serve, store intent/ack, two content-store puts (recovery first, body second), fence, offer; the authority's commit; the reply journal; and composition ticks. They are meaningful at the granularity of **one store put per object**.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides).** Store the pack as **one** content-store object. Then every cut keeps its meaning: `:export/store-put-*` cuts the recovery v2 put, `:export/body-put-*` the pack put, and a pack is either whole or absent; a torn pack is impossible by the store's atomic insert. Storing rows individually (one object per row, Merkle-addressed) would make partial checkpoints possible and require a new cut family (`:pack/missing-row-before-fence`) and a new refusal path (`rows/decode` `:missing-row` at the authority), with no benefit in phase 7. Row-level storage is a phase-8+ option for deduplication across checkpoints and is out of scope. One new cut is worth adding cheaply: `:export/pack-put-after-recovery-missing` is already covered by `store-put-before` (recovery absent, nothing fenced); no addition needed.\n\n**Cost.** None beyond keeping the two-put order in `export-fence-step`.\n\n### 3.5 Publication of successors\n\n**Finding.** The protocol (`exit-mint` → prepare → freeze → fence → report → release → closure; the successor offer refused `:awaiting-completion` then admitted) is body-agnostic except for three values: the successor header (built from the machine's `:yin.k/custody`), the report's result address and the stored bytes.\n\n**Recommendation (decided).** No protocol change. The successor's header takes `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from the machine's custody map as today; the lift writes it into the slot; the inspector's `:counter-regression` check compares slot against baseline. The report carries the successor's pack address; `reopen-exit` rebuilds from the journaled mint and the fence as today. The one wrinkle: `reopen-holder` and `rebuilt-cell` read the kind and occurrence from the stored bytes through the inspector; the rows inspector answers the same keys.\n\n**Cost.** Included in 7b.\n\n### 3.6 Authority and capability tokens versus sealed refs on rows\n\n**Finding.** Rows re-seal every stream and cursor-ref under the receiver's owner on lower (`engine/issue-ref`), authenticate refs on lift, and carry no credential (UCF 7.7.8: \"the envelope carries no credential; the holder is authenticated by the attribution the composition supplies\"). The owner ruled (2026-10-12) that ShiBi tokens are a configurable policy at the `:admit` seam, and (2026-09-27) that ShiBi is a tuple space emerging from index and query interpreters, with `dao.stream.remote` offering only a capability-agnostic seam.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides; owner to confirm scope).** Phase 7 carries **no** capability token in any row and adds no row kind for one. Authority is ledger facts, attribution is the composition's resolver, tenure is lease evidence; sealed refs are the receiver's and are minted on lower. A token-bearing admission is ShiBi work at the admission seam, after phase 8.\n\n**Cost.** None.\n\n### 3.7 `:resume-mode` under version 2\n\n**Finding.** The owner's ruling (orchestrator log, 2026-10-11) is that the version-2 stack lane stays frozen and does not carry `:resume-mode`. Rows carry `:act` and `:tail` rows; a `:tail` is the register kernel's `:return-result` delivery and an `:act` the `:write-result` one (continuation §4.2, §4.3), so the information `:resume-mode` encodes is structural in version 3.\n\n**Recommendation (decided).** No work. Phase 7 does not touch version 2; version 3 needs no such key.\n\n### 3.8 Two gaps the brief did not list but phase 7 meets\n\n- **Continuation §11 Q4 (op-id rules on lower).** Closed by the rows inspector running inside `lower-task`'s custody stage for exclusive roots (below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent without origin, never the body's own occurrence). Fork roots refuse any op-id (`:fork-op-id`), as `inspect-v2` does. Decided, in 7a.\n- **Continuation §11 Q3 (install verification).** `check-install!` verifies status, manifest name, exports and that the image loads, not link-id = parent nor image address = the manifest's. Adding both is about a quarter day in 7b. **OWNER decides** (§3.10 Q3); recommend yes, because the recovery object now trusts the pack for install children.\n- **Continuation §11 Q1/Q10 (the `:running` wire phase).** Unchanged by phase 7; Stage E's installer scenarios use phase `:parked` only. Leave open.\n\n### 3.9 Which kernels take part\n\nStage E runs the **semantic-register kernel only** (the reference lane). The de Bruijn kernels are covered by the cross-engine laws of the harness (61/350, accepted) and are not Stage E holders or sources; the holder driver never lowers through a layout. This follows the owner's matrix ruling and avoids three times the process matrix.\n\n### 3.10 The owner's questions (blocking)\n\n- **Q1, fork spelling in the custody slot.** nil (landed; recommended) or the draft's `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` (half a day of pin regeneration; the draft is amended either way).\n- **Q2, the REPL custody gap.** `yin.repl` composes the **old** `yin.vm.semantic` kernel (`repl.cljc:144`) and wires `compose` (`repl/main.cljc:979-1011`). After phase 7 the driver reads only packs, so the REPL's custody path cannot lift its machine until phase 8 renames the semantic-register kernel into `yin.vm.semantic`. Options: (a) accept the gap until 8 (recommended: `the-plain-composition-loads-and-drives-with-no-yin-repl-test` is re-based on a semantic-register receiver, and the REPL's `:custody` branch is marked inert until 8); (b) pull the REPL's kernel switch into phase 7 (an extra slice, two to three days, touching `yin.repl` and its tests, which 4b/5 did not plan).\n- **Q3, the install checks of continuation §11 Q3** in 7b (recommended yes, a quarter day).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Slicing\n\nEach slice: files, dependencies, acceptance tests, hosts, days (opus implementation + review rounds at the observed 2-4 codex rounds per slice, about half a day per round including fixes).\n\n### 7a: custody on rows, the rows inspector, the authority's fixtures\n\n**Create.** `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows/checkpoint.cljc` (the inspector of §3.3); `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc` (semantic-register machine builders mirroring `lift_support`: `new-machine` over `sr/create-vm` with `tu/make-stream`, deterministic one-slot streams, `parked-reader`, `parked-writer`, `parked-explicit`, `parked-installer`, `parked-linker`, `halted-machine`, `halted-with-cursors`, `parked-module-store-cursors`, `with-op-id`, `serve-table`, `header`, `source-index` capture through `linearize/project`); `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc` + `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` (the five bases lifted by `rows/lift-task` under a header, the mutations as raw row edits with rehash where they touch a row and as root-slot edits otherwise, three anchors with full pack bytes); `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_test.cljc`; `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` (the lift and lower custody rules of §3.1: header by kind, the three lift refusals, the seven grant checks with zero attachments, protection in both directions, exact counter restore, halted exclusive lowers ended, fork under `:exclusive` refused).\n\n**Change.** `rows.cljc` (`emit` sorts rows by id; `lift-task` `:header`; `lower-task` custody stage with `:grant`/`:protection`/`:exclusive`/`:fenced` opts; `check-custody!` replaced); `authority/grant.cljc`, `authority/completion.cljc`, `authority/admission.cljc` (inspector call); `ledger.cljc` (none expected; `:yin.k/root` in the baseline is opaque to the fold); `ledger_fixtures.cljc` (`park`/`halted` from rows packs; `path` → `ledger-v2.txt`), `ledger_fixture_test`, `crash_cut_test`, `grant_test`, `completion_test`, `inherited_test`, `front_test` (fixture bodies only).\n\n**Depends on.** Nothing; owner's Q1.\n\n**Acceptance.** `rows_checkpoint_test` (every base accepted with its baseline; every mutation refused at its path; the file equals `render` on three hosts), `rows_custody_test`, `ledger_fixture_test` (every host rebuilds every frame byte for byte; the projection digest pinned), `crash_cut_test`, the eight authority tests green, `rows_test` and the safepoint harness unchanged (no root id moves), `store_write_audit_test` allowlist updated if the row table changes.\n\n**Hosts.** JVM, Node, Dart.\n\n**Days.** 2.5 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) = **4**.\n\n### 7b: the holder's export record, recovery v2, the driver's lower\n\n**Change.** `holder/export.cljc` (`prepare`/`encode` over `rows/lift-task` with `:header` and a `:serve-keyed` adaptor; `freeze` → recovery v2; `rehydrate-fenced` over `lower-task {:fenced true}`); `holder/driver.cljc` (`lower`, `step-validating`, `rebuilt-cell`, `reopen-holder`, `reopen-exit`, `source-mint`/`exit-mint` unchanged in shape; `:export-version` removed); `compose.cljc` (`holder-config-keys`/`driver-config` drop `:export-version`); `holder/export_test`, `holder/driver_test`, `holder/writer_test`, `holder/reader_test` (machines from `rows_support`; rec1 rows replaced; the v2 fork-lift deftest deleted); `handoff_v2_holder_test` (one deftest deleted).\n\n**Depends on.** 7a; owner's Q3 for the install checks (a quarter day inside this slice).\n\n**Acceptance.** `export_test` (recovery v2 freezes and rehydrates fenced with zero program IO, the published pack reproduced byte for byte, aliases and distinct cursor cells preserved, install children rehydrated without initialization or IO, a restarted abort refused), `driver_test` whole (117 deftests), `writer_test`, `reader_test`, `evidence_test`; the law \"zero attachments before the custody stage refuses\" pinned by attach counts.\n\n**Hosts.** JVM, Node, Dart.\n\n**Days.** 3 implementation + 2 review (4 rounds; the driver is 2.9k lines and its tests 4.3k) = **5**.\n\n### 7c: the composition and the compose and fencing rows\n\n**Change.** `fencing_rows_test` (rows lift under header, `lower-task` with grant opts), `compose_test` (semantic-register `:receiver`/`:machine`; the plain-composition test per Q2), `compose_rows_test` (41 deftests; `offer-variant`-style helpers re-emit packs; content-store cuts see recovery v2 then the pack); `yin.repl/main.cljc` only if Q2 answers (b).\n\n**Depends on.** 7b; owner's Q2.\n\n**Acceptance.** All three files green on three hosts; the slow deftest `completion-recovery-unknown-policy-transaction-is-reconciled-once-test` under `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1` on each lane.\n\n**Days.** 1.5 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) = **3**.\n\n### 7d: Stage E on the semantic-register kernel, three hosts, four process pairs\n\n**Change.** `stage_e_world.cljc` (`parked-reader-over`, `reading-writer` over `rows_support`; `holder-seams` supplies `:source-index`; `offer-variant!` re-emits; `snapshot`'s `:fences` reads pack digests), `stage_e_scenarios.cljc` (checks that name `:yin.k/id-counter` or body keys adjusted; row 1's \"two encodings\" assertion keeps its meaning), `stage_e_peer.cljc` (none expected), `stage_e_process_test.clj` (none expected; the Node peer build picks up the new namespaces), `stage_e_rows_test.cljc` (none expected).\n\n**Depends on.** 7c (the world port can begin on 7b and run in-process while 7c is in review).\n\n**Acceptance.** `stage_e_rows_test` on JVM, Node and Dart (slow tier, `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`); `stage_e_process_test` on the JVM over `[[:jvm :jvm] [:jvm :node] [:node :jvm] [:node :node]]`, every life its own process, every cut a SIGKILL, `target/stage-e-durability.edn` appended; the fast-lane gate `the-cross-host-handoff-across-processes-test` first. Row 10's canonical-byte agreement across hosts now holds for packs as well (sorted rows).\n\n**Hosts.** JVM, Node, Dart (in-process); JVM↔Node (processes).\n\n**Days.** 2 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) + 0.5 for the slow matrix runs (the process matrix is about an hour per full pass on the remote hosts, per build-n-test) = **4**.\n\n### 7e: documentation\n\n**Change.** `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §2.5, §2.6 (custody slot by kind), §7.1-7.2 (the `:header` lift, the custody and fenced lower stages), §8.4 (phase 7 met), §10 (a 10.7 inventory), §11 (Q3, Q4 closed; Q1/Q10 left); `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §5.5 (the custody table as ruled), §7 (pack address versus root id, sorted rows), §9 (the fenced lower); `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` 7.7.4 (recovery v2 paragraph replacing the version-1 one), 7.11.1 clauses 1-5 and 10 (stage letters updated); `docs/orchestrator-log.md` entry.\n\n**Depends on.** The owner's answers; concurrent with 7c/7d.\n\n**Days.** 1 + 1 review round = **1.5**.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Risks and what could force a redesign\n\n1. **The identity split (pack address versus root id).** If the owner or a reviewer insists the ledger's `:yin.k/id` be the root id, the content store contract must change (a verifier-taking put) or the authority must store under the pack address and record both; the second is what §3.3 recommends anyway, so the redesign cost is in naming, not mechanics. Sorting rows removes the host-order variance that would otherwise make JVM and Node packs distinct variants.\n2. **Fenced rehydration on the semantic-register kernel.** `rehydrate-fenced-body` relies on the version-0/1/2 lower's `:fenced?` arm (`handoff.cljc:3688-3699`, `:3722-3725`): gate `:exporting`, waits parked in `:yin.k/recovery-waits`, `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids`, the FFI pair resources for `:ffi-request` pendings, `id-counter` carried exactly. `lower-task` has none of this; porting it is routine but the install-child case (children exporting, their parked records moved to the child's record) is exercised only by `export_test`. Medium.\n3. **Install children on the semantic-register kernel under the holder.** The harness proves rows 7-12 (4b stage 2) for lift/lower; the holder's reader (held observations, replay through child call ids) over semantic-register install children has no test today. If `reader.cljc`'s walk assumes a field the new kernel spells differently, 7b finds it. Low-medium: both kernels share `engine.cljc` for `:installs`, `:wait-set`, `:yin.k/held`.\n4. **The REPL gap (Q2).** If the owner wants the REPL's custody demo live between 7 and 8, phase 7 grows by a slice and touches the 4b/5 \"no REPL edit\" boundary.\n5. **Stage E's pinned row-5 behaviour** (\"the exposed gap marker is the first and only commit\", pending an Architect ruling) is inherited unchanged; a ruling during phase 7 would change `row5`'s check, not the transport.\n6. **Dart.** Stage E runs in-process only on Dart (as today); the numbering budget (`default-budget`) is far above Stage E's task sizes. The known Dart traps (reader whitespace, `for` over chunked seqs, multi-key `assoc` on nil, duplicate protocol params) apply to the new support namespace; the harness already avoids them.\n7. **Review load.** Phase 6b took three codex rounds on a 1.8k-line file; 7b touches the 2.9k-line driver through eight call sites plus `export.cljc` whole. Budgeted at four rounds.\n8. **What would force a redesign of rows:** a requirement that the body's identity be a byte address (contradicts Merkle rows; §3.3 resolves it with two identities); a requirement to carry lease or epoch in the body (contradicts UCF 7.2.1, not proposed); row-level storage with partial checkpoints (deferred, §3.4). None is live.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Order of work and concurrency\n\n```\n7a (custody, inspector, fixtures)        ──────────┐\n                                                   ▼\n7b (export record, recovery v2, driver)  ──────────┐\n                                                   ▼\n7c (compose, compose/fencing rows)       ──────┐   ├── 7d world port may start here\n                                               ▼   ▼\n7d (Stage E, in-process three hosts, then the process matrix)\n7e (docs) ─ starts after the owner's answers, lands beside 7c/7d\n```\n\n- 7a is the only slice that needs an owner answer before it starts (Q1). Q2 gates one deftest in 7c and possibly an extra slice; Q3 gates a quarter day in 7b.\n- Concurrent work: 7e with 7c/7d; the 7d world port (`stage_e_world` over `rows_support`) with 7c's review, since it depends on 7b's `export`/`driver` only. The old-codec tests are never touched, so no conflict with other worktrees; `rows.cljc` is the one file 7a and the harness share, and 7a keeps every pinned root id.\n- Landing cadence (owner's tiers): quick tier at every checkpoint; the slow tier (Stage E, the completion-recovery slow deftest, the process matrix) before each merge of 7c and 7d and once at the phase boundary; one lane set per machine, Dart in its own clone on the remote host.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Honest total\n\n| Slice | Implementation (opus) | Review rounds | Days |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 7a | 2.5 | 3 | 4 |\n| 7b | 3 | 4 | 5 |\n| 7c | 1.5 | 3 | 3 |\n| 7d | 2 | 3 (+0.5 matrix runs) | 4 |\n| 7e | 1 | 1 | 1.5 |\n| **Total** | **10** | | **17.5 sequential** |\n\nWith 7e concurrent and the 7d port overlapping 7c, the critical path is 7a → 7b → 7c → 7d: about **14 days** of work; **12 days median** if reviews average the lower end (two rounds on 7a/7c), **17 at the worst** (four rounds everywhere, one redesign loop on the identity split). Phase 6's three slices went from first commit to merge in three calendar days with the design already written and reviews back-to-back; phase 7 has four code slices over twice the surface, so calendar time of one to two weeks is realistic if codex capacity holds.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict\n\n**NOT READY.** Blocking questions for the owner: **Q1** fork spelling in the custody slot (nil, recommended, or the draft's explicit map); **Q2** the REPL custody gap until phase 8 (accept, recommended, or pull the REPL kernel switch into phase 7); **Q3** the install link-id and image-address checks in 7b (recommended yes). Every other design point above is decided by the Architect within the owner's rulings and is ready for an implementation brief for 7a on receipt of Q1.\n","numLines":320,"startLine":1,"totalLines":320}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01S2oMr3noAsXjpNipQD1hmo","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EYhuiNmoXiS8kNXtMJNsX2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n3\t---\n4\t\n5\t# BUILD & TEST\n6\t\n7\t## Commands\n8\t\n9\t```sh\n10\t# Tests (Babashka runner)\n11\tbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\n12\tbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\n13\tbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n14\t# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\n15\tbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\n16\tbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\n17\tbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\n18\tbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\n19\tbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n20\tbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n21\tbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\n22\tbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n23\tbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n24\t# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n25\t# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n26\t# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n27\t# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n28\t# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n29\t# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n30\t# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n31\t# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n32\t# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n33\t# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n34\t# which lanes may overlap.\n35\t#\n36\t# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n37\t# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n38\t# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n39\t# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n40\t# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n41\t# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n42\t# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n43\t# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n44\t# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n45\t# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n46\t# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n47\t# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n48\t# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n49\t# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n50\t# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n51\t# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n52\t# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n53\t# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n54\t# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n55\t# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n56\t# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n57\t# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n58\t# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n59\t# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\n60\tnpm test             # Node.js tests\n61\t\n62\t# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n63\t# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n64\t# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n65\t# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\n66\tbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\n67\tbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n68\t                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n69\t                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n70\t                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n71\t```\n72\t\n73\t### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n74\t\n75\t`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\n76\treach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\n77\tmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\n78\ttests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\n79\tA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\n80\tfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\n81\tio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n82\t(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\n83\tlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\n84\tneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n85\t`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\n86\ttypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\n87\tland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\n88\tchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\n89\tantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\n90\tas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\n91\tdao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n92\t(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\n93\tand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n94\t\n95\t### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n96\t\n97\t`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\n98\tone or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\n99\tis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n100\t\n101\t```sh\n102\tbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\n103\tbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\n104\tbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\n105\tbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\n106\tbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\n107\tbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\n108\tbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n109\t```\n110\t\n111\t#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n112\t\n113\t- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n114\t- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n115\t- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n116\t- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n117\t- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n118\t- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n119\t\n120\tPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\n121\tand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\n122\tselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n123\t\n124\t#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n125\t\n126\t1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n127\t2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n128\t3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n129\t4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n130\t5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n131\t\n132\t\n133\tThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\n134\tslice L5) is two tests:\n135\t\n136\t- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n137\t  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n138\t  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n139\t  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n140\t  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n141\t  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n142\t  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n143\t  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n144\t- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n145\t  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n146\t  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n147\t  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n148\t  REPL.\n149\t\n150\t```sh\n151\t# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\n152\tclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\n153\tclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\n154\tclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n155\t\n156\t# ClojureDart\n157\tclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n158\t\n159\t# Linting\n160\tclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n161\t```\n162\t\n163\tThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n164\t[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\n165\tnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\n166\tDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\n167\twith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n168\t`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\n169\texits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\n170\teach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\n171\tscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n172\t<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n173\t(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n174\t`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\n175\tunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\n176\tdifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\n177\tpackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n178\t\n179\t`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\n180\tcompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\n181\taggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\n182\tstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\n183\tbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n184\t\n185\t## Test tiers: quick and slow\n186\t\n187\tThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n188\t(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n189\t\n190\t- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n191\t  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n192\t  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n193\t- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n194\t  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n195\t- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n196\t  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n197\t  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n198\t  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n199\t  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n200\t  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n201\t  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n202\t  with the method below before changing the cut.\n203\t- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\n204\t  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n205\t  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\n206\t\n207\tMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\n208\tservers\" for how to run them):\n209\t\n210\t| Run | Time |\n211\t|---|---|\n212\t| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n213\t| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n214\t| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n215\t| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n216\t\n217\tThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\n218\tSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n219\t`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\n220\tunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\n221\tA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n222\t\n223\t**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\n224\trun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\n225\totherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n226\t\n227\t```sh\n228\tNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n229\t# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n230\t#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n231\t# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n232\t# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n233\t```\n234\t\n235\t**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\n236\tshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\n237\ttests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\n238\tJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n239\t\n240\t```sh\n241\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\n242\t# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\n243\tset -u\n244\texport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\n245\tcd \"$1\" || exit 2\n246\tPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\n247\tmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\n248\teval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n249\tOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\n250\tts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\n251\techo \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n252\tnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n253\tbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n254\tbb gen:python-antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n255\tSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n256\t: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n257\tfor s in $SUBS; do\n258\t  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n259\t  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n260\t  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\n261\tdone\n262\t# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\n263\tfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\n264\tsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\n265\tcomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\n266\techo \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n267\trun_shard() {\n268\t  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n269\t  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n270\t  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n271\t  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n272\t}\n273\texport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\n274\tprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\n275\techo \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n276\t```\n277\t\n278\t**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n279\t\n280\t```sh\n281\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\n282\t# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\n283\tset -u\n284\texport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\n285\tcd \"$1\" || exit 2\n286\tPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\n287\tmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\n288\teval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n289\tOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\n290\trun_ns() {\n291\t  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n292\t  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n293\t  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n294\t}\n295\texport -f run_ns; export OUT\n296\techo \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n297\txargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\n298\techo \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n299\t```\n300\t\n301\t## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n302\t\n303\tTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\n304\treached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\n305\tand `git`.\n306\t\n307\t| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (checked 2026-10-11) |\n308\t|---|---|---|\n309\t| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Complete: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1-stable. All three lanes run. |\n310\t| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | java 21, clojure, node, zprint; babashka installed with `mise install babashka@latest`; flutter 3.41.9 is NOT the pinned version. Run the JVM and Node lanes here, not Dart. |\n311\t\n312\t**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\n313\tthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\n314\towner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n315\t\n316\t### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n317\t\n318\tRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\n319\twould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\n320\tdirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\n321\tbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\n322\tthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n323\t\n324\t```sh\n325\t# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\n326\tssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n327\t  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n328\t  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n329\t  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n330\t  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n331\t```\n332\t\n333\tTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n334\t`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\n335\twas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n336\t\n337\t### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n338\t\n339\tA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\n340\ttools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\n341\tinto the clone:\n342\t\n343\t```sh\n344\texport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\n345\tcd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\n346\tmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\n347\teval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-pinned tools on PATH\n348\t```\n349\t\n350\tActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-pinned tools\n351\t(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\n352\tfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n353\t\n354\t### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n355\t\n356\t\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\n357\tCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n358\t`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\n359\tavoidable\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\n360\tlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n361\t\n362\t- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n363\t  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n364\t  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n365\t  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n366\t  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n367\t  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n368\t  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n369\t  verified by a measured run.\n370\t- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n371\t  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n372\t- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n373\t  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n374\t- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n375\t  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n376\t- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n377\t\n378\tThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\n379\tthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\n380\tseparate Dart clone.\n381\t\n382\t### The full run\n383\t\n384\tRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\n385\tserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\n386\tthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\n387\ttasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\n388\tlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\n389\tsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n390\t\n391\t```sh\n392\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\n393\t# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\n394\tset -u\n395\texport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\n396\tDART=\"${2:-}\"\n397\tcd \"$1\" || exit 2\n398\tmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\n399\teval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n400\tLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\n401\tts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\n402\techo \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n403\tnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n404\tbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n405\tbb gen:python-antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n406\tbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"  # shiva only\n407\tlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n408\t         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\n409\tlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\n410\tlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\n411\tif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n412\t  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n413\t    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n414\t    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\n415\tfi\n416\twait\n417\techo \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n418\t```\n419\t\n420\t```sh\n421\tssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n422\t```\n423\t\n424\tWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\n425\tpress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\n426\tand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\n427\twrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n428\t`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\n429\tscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n430\t(measured 2026-10-11).\n431\t\n432\t### Known differences from the laptop\n433\t\n434\t- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\n435\t  The assertion \"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\" gets no `bind-failed`\n436\t  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\n437\t  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\n438\t  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\n439\t  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\n440\t- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n441\t  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n442\t- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n443\t  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n444\t\n445\t### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n446\t\n447\tShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\n448\tit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\n449\tNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\n450\tnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\n451\tlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\n452\textra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\n453\t\n454\t## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n455\t\n456\tWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n457\t\n458\tTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n459\t\n460\tWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n461\t1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n462\t2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n463\t3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n464\t\n465\tThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n466\t\n467\t## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n468\t\n469\tA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n470\t\n471\t1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n472\t   ```sh\n473\t   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n474\t   ```\n475\t\n476\t2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n477\t   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n478\t\n479\t3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n480\t   Common patterns:\n481\t   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n482\t   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n483\t   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n484\t\n485\t4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n486\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"108e1cb8-f707-457b-be14-6feb1e135c49","uuid":"113aa9a6-d93a-45f8-801c-be6152f1fcc0","timestamp":"2026-10-13T10:02:41.783Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7b/docs/agents/build-n-test.md","content":"---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\n\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\nservers\" for how to run them):\n\n| Run | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n\n```sh\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n```\n\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\nfor s in $SUBS; do\n  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\ndone\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\nfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\nsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\ncomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\necho \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nrun_shard() {\n  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\nprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nrun_ns() {\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\necho \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nxargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\necho \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\nand `git`.\n\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (checked 2026-10-11) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Complete: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1-stable. All three lanes run. |\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | java 21, clojure, node, zprint; babashka installed with `mise install babashka@latest`; flutter 3.41.9 is NOT the pinned version. Run the JVM and Node lanes here, not Dart. |\n\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n\n```sh\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n```\n\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\ninto the clone:\n\n```sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-pinned tools on PATH\n```\n\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-pinned tools\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\nfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n\n\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\navoidable\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\nseparate Dart clone.\n\n### The full run\n\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\nDART=\"${2:-}\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"  # shiva only\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\nif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\nfi\nwait\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n```sh\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n```\n\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n(measured 2026-10-11).\n\n### Known differences from the laptop\n\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\n  The assertion \"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\" gets no `bind-failed`\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n","numLines":486,"startLine":1,"totalLines":486}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EYhuiNmoXiS8kNXtMJNsX2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
